'''Gordon Douglas Jones''' (born May 4, 1954) is an American politician and attorney who served as a [[United States Senate|United States senator]] from [[Alabama]] from 2018 to 2021.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|date=2022-02-02|title=President Biden Announces Additional Advisors for Supreme Court Process|url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/02/02/president-biden-announces-additional-advisors-for-supreme-court-process/|access-date=2022-02-24|website=The White House|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Doug Jones (@DougJones46) |url=https://twitter.com/dougjones46 |website=Twitter |access-date=28 March 2022}}</ref> A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], Jones was previously the [[United States attorney]] for the [[United States District Court for the Northern District of Alabama|Northern District of Alabama]] from 1997 to 2001. As of 2026, he is the last Democrat to have won or held statewide office in Alabama.

Jones was born in [[Fairfield, Alabama]], and is a graduate of the [[University of Alabama]] and [[Cumberland School of Law]] at [[Samford University]]. After law school, he worked as a congressional staffer and as a federal prosecutor before moving to private practice. In 1997, President [[Bill Clinton]] appointed Jones as [[U.S. Attorney]] for the [[Northern District of Alabama]]. Jones's most prominent cases were the successful defense of Tom Posey, [[Ku Klux Klan]] supporter and alleged [[Iran–Contra affair|Iran Contra affair]] co-conspirator and the prosecution of two Ku Klux Klan members for the [[16th Street Baptist Church bombing|1963 Birmingham church bombing]] that killed four African-American girls and the indictment of domestic terrorist [[Eric Rudolph]]. He returned to private practice at the conclusion of Clinton's presidency in 2001.<ref name=":2" /><ref name=":0" /><ref name=":3" />

Jones announced his candidacy for [[United States Senate]] in the [[2017 United States Senate special election in Alabama|2017 special election]] following the resignation of Republican incumbent [[Jeff Sessions]] to become [[United States Attorney General|U.S. Attorney General]]. After winning the Democratic primary in August, he faced former [[Supreme Court of Alabama|Alabama Supreme Court]] Justice [[Roy Moore]] in the general election. Jones was considered a long-shot candidate in a [[Cook Partisan Voting Index|deeply Republican state]]. A month before the election, Moore was [[Roy Moore sexual misconduct allegations|alleged]] to have sexually assaulted and otherwise acted inappropriately with several women, including some who were minors at the time.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2017/dec/12/roy-moore-loses-alabama-senate-race-doug-jones-wins|title=Alabama election: Democrats triumph over Roy Moore in major blow to Trump |last1=Jacobs |first1=Ben |last2=Smith |first2=David |date=December 13, 2017 |newspaper=The Guardian |access-date=August 6, 2018}}</ref> Jones won the special election by 22,000 votes, 50%–48%.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/turbulent-senate-race-now-hands-alabama-voters-51733556 |title=Democrat Jones wins stunning red-state Alabama Senate upset |author1=Chandler, Kim |date=December 13, 2017 |work=ABC News |agency=Associated Press |author2=Peoples, Steve |access-date=December 13, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171217005217/https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/turbulent-senate-race-now-hands-alabama-voters-51733556 |archive-date=December 17, 2017 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Jones ran for a full term in [[2020 United States Senate election in Alabama|2020]] and lost to Republican nominee [[Tommy Tuberville]] in a landslide. His margin of defeat was the largest of an incumbent senator since 2010.<ref name="Clare Foran">{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/11/03/politics/tommy-tuberville-wins-alabama-senate/index.html|title=Democrat Doug Jones loses Alabama Senate seat to Republican Tommy Tuberville|author=Clare Foran|website=CNN|date=November 4, 2020 }}</ref>

In January 2021, he joined [[CNN]] as a political commentator.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Lonas |first1=Lexi |date=January 29, 2021 |title=Doug Jones joining CNN as political commentator |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/media/536475-doug-jones-joining-cnn-as-political-commentator/ |website=The Hill |access-date=February 6, 2021}}</ref> Jones was a fellow at the [[Georgetown University]]'s [[Georgetown Institute of Politics and Public Service|Institute of Politics and Public Service]] during the spring 2021 academic semester, and was a distinguished Pritzker Fellow at the [[University of Chicago]]'s [[University of Chicago Institute of Politics|Institute of Politics]] during the fall of 2022.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Doug Jones|url=https://politics.georgetown.edu/profile/doug-jones/|access-date=2021-04-20|publisher=Georgetown University [[McCourt School of Public Policy]], Institute of Politics and Public Service|language=en-US}}</ref> In February 2022, the [[Biden administration]] named him as a nomination advisor for legislative affairs, advising the president on [[Joe Biden Supreme Court candidates|Supreme Court nominations]].<ref name="auto"/> He is currently the Democratic nominee in the [[2026 Alabama gubernatorial election]].

==Early life and education==
Doug Jones was born in [[Fairfield, Alabama]], to Gordon and Gloria (Wesson) Jones.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://dougjonesforsenate.com/|title=Meet Doug Jones": "Doug's parents, Gordon and Gloria Jones, live in Birmingham and his sister Terrie Savage and her husband Scott live in Hartselle.|publisher=Doug Jones for Senate|date=August 16, 2017|access-date=August 16, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171212011108/https://dougjonesforsenate.com/|archive-date=December 12, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://obits.al.com/amp/obituaries/birmingham/194881872|title=Gordon Jones Obituary (1931 - 2019) - The Birmingham News|website=obits.al.com}}</ref> His father worked at [[U.S. Steel]] and his mother was a homemaker.<ref>{{Citation |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/05/05/us/public-lives-an-alabama-prosecutor-confronts-the-burden-of-history.html|title=PUBLIC LIVES; An Alabama Prosecutor Confronts the Burden of History|last=Sack|first=Kevin|date=May 5, 2001|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref> He went to [[Fairfield High Preparatory School|Fairfield High School]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.newsweek.com/doug-jones-roy-more-alabama-senate-743312 | title=Who is Doug Jones? Alabama Senate Race Heats up | website=[[Newsweek]] | date=December 10, 2017 }}</ref> Jones graduated from the [[University of Alabama]] with a [[Bachelor of Science]] in political science in 1976, and earned his [[Juris Doctor]] from [[Cumberland School of Law]] at [[Samford University]] in 1979. He is a member of [[Beta Theta Pi]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cobb |first1=Martin |title=Brother Senator |url=https://issuu.com/betathetapiao/docs/beta_theta_pi_sping_2018/10 |work=The Beta Theta Pi |date=Spring 2018 |page=10 |access-date=June 18, 2018 }}</ref>

Jones's political career began as staff counsel to the [[U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee]] for Alabama Senator [[Howell Heflin]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.al.com/news/index.ssf/2017/05/doug_jones_announces_run_for_u.html|title=Doug Jones announces run for US Senate|last=Gray|first=Jeremy|date=May 11, 2017 |newspaper=[[The Birmingham News]] |access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref> Jones then worked as an [[Assistant U.S. Attorney]] from 1980 to 1984 before resigning to work at a private law firm in [[Birmingham, Alabama]], from 1984 to 1997.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.publicjustice.org/uploads/file/pdf/Murnaghan_Lecture_flyer_Douglas_Jones_bio.pdf|title=Douglas Jones|last=Murnaghan|publisher=Public Justice|access-date=May 18, 2017|archive-date=December 13, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171213225318/http://www.publicjustice.org/uploads/file/pdf/Murnaghan_Lecture_flyer_Douglas_Jones_bio.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> He ran in the Democratic primary for district 6 of the [[Alabama House of Representatives]] in 1994, but did not advance to the runoff.<ref>1994 Election Results Archive - Alabama Legislature. Available at: https://www.sos.alabama.gov/alabama-votes/voter/election-data. Retrieved 5 August 2024.</ref>

==Career==

=== Defense of Tom Posey in Iran Contra affair ===
In 1988, Jones acted as defense counsel for Alabama man Tom Posey  who allegedly supplied weapons to [[Contras|Contras in Nicaragua]] through his organization Civilian Military Assistance with weapons and was a well-known supporter of the [[Ku Klux Klan|KKK]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Images |first=Historic |title=1989 Press Photo Doug Jones, Tom Posey, press conference, Samford Univ., Alabama |url=https://historicimages.com/products/abna37442 |access-date=2025-05-02 |website=Historic Images |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Thornton |first=William |date=2017-12-12 |title=Robocall accuses Doug Jones of defending KKK supporter |url=https://www.al.com/news/2017/12/robocall_accuses_doug_jones_of.html |access-date=2025-05-02 |website=al |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite web |title=Alabama mercenary meets with investigators today - UPI Archives |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1987/04/23/Alabama-mercenary-meets-with-investigators-today/6947546148800/ |access-date=2025-10-31 |website=UPI |language=en}}</ref>

During the 2017 Special Senate election, this history served as the basis for an opposition campaign, calling into question Jones's support for civil rights and the black community.<ref name=":0" />

=== Joe Biden 1988 presidential campaign ===
In 1988, Jones served as co-chair for [[Joe Biden 1988 presidential campaign|Joe Biden's 1988 presidential bid]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Gaudiano |first=Nicole Gaudiano and Nicole |title=Doug Jones helps Joe Biden raise money for his PAC, citing old friendship |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/onpolitics/2017/12/19/jones-touts-friendship-biden-fundraising-email/966773001/ |access-date=2025-05-03 |website=USA TODAY |language=en-US}}</ref>  Biden and Jones have remained friends since first meeting around 1976.<ref name=":1" />

=== U.S. Attorney appointment ===
President [[Bill Clinton]] announced on August 18, 1997, his intent to appoint Jones as [[U.S. Attorney]] for the [[Northern District of Alabama]],<ref>{{Cite press release|url=https://clinton6.nara.gov/1997/08/1997-08-18-douglas-jones-nominated-as-us-attorney-in-alabama.html|title=President Clinton today announced his intent to nominate G. Douglas Jones to serve as United States Attorney for the Northern District of Alabama.|publisher=[[White House Office of the Press Secretary]]|date=August 18, 1997|access-date=May 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111025122641/http://clinton6.nara.gov/1997/08/1997-08-18-douglas-jones-nominated-as-us-attorney-in-alabama.html|archive-date=October 25, 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref> and formally nominated Jones to the post on September 2, 1997.<ref name="GSU">[http://readingroom.law.gsu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1745&context=gsulr Panel Discussion: Criminal Discovery In Practice], 15 [[Georgia State University Law Review|Ga. St. U. L. Rev.]] 781, 782 n.2 (1999).</ref> On September 8, 1997, the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Alabama appointed Jones as interim U.S. Attorney. The [[United States Senate|Senate]] confirmed Jones' nomination on November 8, 1997,<ref name="GSU" /> by [[voice vote]].<ref>{{cite news |first=John |last=Verhoevek |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/alabama-senate-race-pits-doug-jones-roy-moore/story?id=50127528 |title=Meet the Alabama Senate candidates: Controversial gun-toting judge Roy Moore and a lawyer who fought the KKK |work=ABC News |date=September 27, 2017}}</ref> Jones served until May 31, 2001.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Jones |first=Doug |title=Bending Toward Justice: The Birmingham Church Bombing That Changed the Course of Civil Rights |publisher=All Points Books |year=2019 |isbn=9781250201447 |location=New York |pages=218}}</ref>

=== Eric Rudolph case ===
In January 1998, [[Eric Rudolph]] bombed the New Woman All Women Health Care Center in Birmingham. Jones was responsible for coordinating the state and federal task force in the aftermath, and advocated that Rudolph be tried first in Birmingham before being extradited and tried in Georgia for his crimes in that state, such as the [[Centennial Olympic Park bombing]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://abcnews.go.com/US/story?id=90596&page=1|title=Bombing Suspect Eric Rudolph Indicted|date=November 15, 2000|work=ABC News|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2003/06/03/us/bombing-suspect-is-moved-to-alabama-for-trial-there-first.html|title=Bombing Suspect Is Moved to Alabama, for Trial There First|last=Gettlman|first=Jeffrey|date=June 3, 2003|newspaper=The New York Times}}</ref>

===16th Street Baptist Church bombing case===
Jones prosecuted [[Thomas Edwin Blanton Jr.]] and [[Bobby Frank Cherry]], two members of the [[Ku Klux Klan]], for their roles in the 1963 [[16th Street Baptist Church bombing]]. The case was reopened the year before Jones was appointed, but did not gain traction until his appointment. A [[federal grand jury]] was called in 1998, which caught the attention of Cherry's ex-wife, Willadean Cherry, and led her to call the FBI to give her testimony. Willadean then introduced Jones to family and friends, who reported their own experiences from the time of the bombing. A key piece of evidence was a tape from the time of the bombing in which Blanton said he had plotted with others to make the bomb. Jones was deputized to argue in state court and indicted Blanton and Cherry in 2000.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/04/25/us/church-bombing-trial-begins-birmingham-city-s-past-very-much-present.html|title=As Church Bombing Trial Begins in Birmingham, the City's Past Is Very Much Present|last=Sack|first=Kevin|date=April 25, 2001 |newspaper=The New York Times|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.latimes.com/nation/la-xpm-2013-sep-14-la-na-nn-birmingham-bombing-prosecutor-20130913-story.html|title=Prosecutor reflects on 50th anniversary of 1963 Birmingham bombing|last=Hennessy-Fiske|first=Molly|date=September 14, 2013 |newspaper=Los Angeles Times|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref> Blanton was found guilty in 2001 and Cherry in 2002. Both were sentenced to life in prison. Cherry died in prison in 2004.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/articles/A61428-2004Nov18.html|title=Birmingham Bomber Bobby Frank Cherry Dies in Prison at 74|last=Lamb|first=Yvonne|date=November 19, 2004|newspaper=The Washington Post|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref> Blanton was up for parole in 2016; Jones spoke against his release, and parole was denied.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.al.com/news/birmingham/index.ssf/2016/08/alabama_board_considering_paro.html|title=16th Street Baptist Church bomber Thomas Blanton denied parole|last=Faulk|first=Kent|date=August 3, 2016|newspaper=[[The Birmingham News]] |access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref> Blanton died in prison in 2020.<ref>{{cite web |title=Thomas Blanton, 16th Street Baptist Church bomber, dies in prison |url=https://www.wbrc.com/2020/06/26/thomas-blanton-th-street-baptist-church-bomber-dies-prison |publisher=wbrc.com |date=June 26, 2020 |access-date=September 18, 2023}}</ref>
{{external media| float = left| video1 = [https://www.c-span.org/video/?457874-1/after-words-senator-doug-jones ''After Words'' interview with Jones on ''Bending Toward Justice'', March 9, 2019], [[C-SPAN]]}}
Jones recounts the history of the bombings and his subsequent involvement in Blanton and Cherry's prosecution in his 2019 book ''Bending Toward Justice: The Birmingham Church Bombing that Changed the Course of Civil Rights''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Sen. Doug Jones to release book in 2019 |url=https://www.al.com/entertainment/2018/04/sen_doug_jones_to_release_book.html |publisher=AL.com |date=April 10, 2018 |access-date=January 7, 2019}}</ref>

=== Return to private practice ===
Jones left office in 2001 and returned to private practice, joining the law firm of Haskell Slaughter Young & Rediker.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.al.com/spotnews/2013/06/birmingham_attorneys_doug_jone.html|title=Birmingham attorneys Doug Jones and Greg Hawley form law firm|date=June 7, 2013|website=al}}</ref> In 2004, he was court-appointed General Special Master in an environmental cleanup case involving [[Monsanto]] in [[Anniston, Alabama]].<ref name="Birmingham attorneys"/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://law.uky.edu/news/doug-jones-justice-delayed-not-justice-denied|title=Doug Jones: Justice Delayed, not Justice Denied|publisher=University of Kentucky Law School|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://seekingjusticetoday.com/about-doug-jones/|title=About Doug Jones|publisher=Seeking Justice Today|access-date=May 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171210070913/http://seekingjusticetoday.com/about-doug-jones/|archive-date=December 10, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> In 2007, the [[Birmingham Civil Rights Institute]] gave Jones its 15th Anniversary Civil Rights Distinguished Service Award.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.texarkanagazette.com/news/texarkana/story/2014/jan/26/federal-prosecutor-speak-black-history-groups/356905/|title=Federal prosecutor to speak at black history group's banquet |date=January 26, 2014|work=Texarkana Gazette|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref> Also in 2007, Jones testified before the [[United States House Committee on the Judiciary]] about the importance of reexamining crimes of the Civil Rights Era.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://judiciary.house.gov/_files/hearings/June2007/061207jones.pdf|title=Testimony of G. Douglas Jones|date=June 12, 2007|publisher=U.S. House Judiciary Committee|access-date=May 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170507174429/https://judiciary.house.gov/_files/hearings/June2007/061207jones.pdf|archive-date=May 7, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://judiciary.house.gov/_files/hearings/pdf/Jones071023.pdf|title=Testimony of G. Douglas Jones-"Allegations of Selective Prosecution: The Erosion of Public Confidence in our Federal Judicial System"|date=October 23, 2007|publisher=Subcommittee on Commercial & Administration Law of the Committee on Judiciary, U.S. House of Representatives|access-date=May 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170507152325/https://judiciary.house.gov/_files/hearings/pdf/Jones071023.pdf|archive-date=May 7, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> In 2013, he formed the Birmingham firm Jones & Hawley, PC with longtime friend Greg Hawley.<ref name="Birmingham attorneys">{{Cite news |url=http://blog.al.com/spotnews/2013/06/birmingham_attorneys_doug_jone.html|title=Birmingham attorneys Doug Jones and Greg Hawley form law firm|last=Faulk|first=Kent|date=June 7, 2013|publisher=AL.com|access-date=May 18, 2017}}</ref> Jones was named one of ''B-Metro Magazine''{{'}}s Fusion Award winners in 2015.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://b-metro.com/fusion2015/23428/|title=2015 Fusion Awards|last=O'Donnell|first=Joe|date=October 1, 2015|work=B-Metro Magazine|access-date=May 18, 2017|archive-date=September 26, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170926144445/http://b-metro.com/fusion2015/23428/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In 2017, he received the Lifetime Achievement Award from the Alabama chapter of the [[Young Democrats of America]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weldbham.com/blog/2017/06/27/democratic-senate-candidate-doug-jones-launches-campaign-headquarters-birmingham/|title=Democratic Senate candidate Doug Jones launches campaign headquarters in Birmingham|work=Weld Birmingham|last=Scott|first=Ryan|date=June 27, 2017|access-date=June 28, 2017}}</ref>

==U.S. Senate==

=== Elections ===

==== 2002 ====
{{Main|2002 United States Senate election in Alabama}}
On August 15, 2001, Jones announced an exploratory candidacy for U.S. Senate against incumbent [[Jeff Sessions]] in [[2002 United States Senate election in Alabama|2002]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2001-08-15 |title=Doug Jones exploring run for U.S. Senate |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/birmingham-post-herald/133527478/ |access-date=2024-10-12 |work=Birmingham Post-Herald |pages=40}}</ref> Jones said he was running because "We've hardly seen Jeff Sessions in the last four years...I'm going to be here to work for the people of Alabama."<ref>{{Cite news |date=2001-08-17 |title=Doug Jones faces long odds in Senate bid (cont.) |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/birmingham-post-herald-doug-jones-faces/157017565/ |access-date=2024-10-12 |work=Birmingham Post-Herald |pages=4}}</ref>  Despite his successful prosecution of the 16th Street Baptist Church Bombing case, Jones was not well-known statewide and had low name recognition.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kizzire |first=Jaime |date=2001-05-07 |title=Could verdict lead to office? |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/birmingham-post-herald-could-verdict-lea/157017750/ |access-date=2024-10-12 |work=Birmingham Post-Herald |pages=23}}</ref> He ended his campaign prior to the Democratic Party primary on February 18, 2002, citing difficulties raising money.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2002-02-19 |title=Doug Jones ends race for U.S. Senate |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-dothan-eagle-doug-jones-ends-race-fo/157017210/ |access-date=2024-10-12 |work=The Dothan Eagle |pages=9}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Gadsden Times - Google News Archive Search |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=cfMwAAAAIBAJ&sjid=7NwFAAAAIBAJ&pg=1237,1516279&dq=jeff+sessions+primary&hl=en |access-date=2025-08-14 |website=news.google.com}}</ref>

==== 2017 ====
{{main|2017 United States Senate special election in Alabama}}

[[File:Doug Jones Biden Event.jpg|thumb|Jones at a campaign rally in October 2017]]
[[File:Vice President Joe Biden Doug Jones.jpg|thumb|Biden rallies for Jones ]]
On May 11, 2017, Jones announced his candidacy for that year's [[2017 United States Senate special election in Alabama|U.S. Senate special election]], running for the seat left open when Sessions was appointed [[Attorney General of the United States|Attorney General]]. Sessions, a Republican, had held the seat since 1997, after Democrat [[Howell Heflin]] chose not to run for reelection.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.al.com/news/index.ssf/2017/05/doug_jones_announces_run_for_u.html|title=Doug Jones announces run for US Senate |newspaper=[[The Birmingham News]] |date=May 10, 2017 |first=Jeremy |last=Gray |access-date=May 10, 2017}}</ref> Jones won the Democratic nomination in August,<ref name="2017 primary results">{{cite news |last1=Bloch|first1=Matthew|last2=Lee|first2=Jasmine|url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/alabama-senate-special-election-primary|title=Alabama Election Results: Two Republicans Advance, Democrat Wins in U.S. Senate Primaries|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=August 15, 2017|access-date=August 15, 2017}}</ref> and became the senator-elect for Alabama after defeating former [[Alabama Supreme Court]] judge [[Roy Moore]] in the general election on December 12, which was also Jones's 25th wedding anniversary.<ref name="Jones wins" /><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://dougjones.com/doug/|title=Meet Doug Jones|publisher=Doug Jones for U.S. Senate|access-date=2018-09-21|archive-date=October 5, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181005164355/https://dougjones.com/doug/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Jones received endorsement from former Vice President [[Joe Biden]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-08-11 |title=Joe Biden endorses candidate in Alabama US Senate race |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/joe-biden-endorses-candidate-alabama-us-senate-race-153029075--election.html |access-date=2026-07-29 |website=Yahoo News |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Press |first=The Associated |date=2017-10-03 |title=Biden to rally support for Jones in Senate race |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/nation-world/biden-to-rally-support-for-jones-in-senate-race |access-date=2026-07-29 |website=WCIV |language=en}}</ref>

Jones received 673,896 votes (50.0%) to Moore's 651,972 votes (48.3%) with 22,852 write-in votes (1.7%).<ref name="Jones wins">{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/alabama-senate-special-election-roy-moore-doug-jones|title=Alabama Election Results: Doug Jones Defeats Roy Moore in U.S. Senate Race|last1=Bloch|first1=Matthew|last2=Cohn|first2=Nate|last3=Katz|first3=Josh|last4=Lee|first4=Jasmine|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=December 12, 2017|access-date=December 12, 2017}}</ref> After the election, Moore refused to concede. He filed a lawsuit attempting to block the state from certifying the election and called for an investigation into voter fraud, as well as a new election.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cnn.com/2017/12/15/politics/trump-and-bannon-urge-roy-moore-to-concede/index.html|title=Trump and Steve Bannon urge Roy Moore to concede|publisher=CNN|date=December 15, 2017|author=Kaitlan Collins}}</ref> On December 28, 2017, a judge dismissed his suit and state officials certified the election results, officially declaring Jones the winner.<ref>{{cite news |last=Nelson |first=Louis |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2017/12/28/roy-moore-lawsuit-alabama-race-doug-jones-319544 |title=Roy Moore loses lawsuit seeking new election |work=Politico |date=December 28, 2017 |access-date=December 28, 2017}}</ref>

==== 2020 ====
{{main|2020 United States Senate election in Alabama}}

[[File:Tommy Tuberville 117th Congress Portrait.jpg|thumb|right|Jones's Republican opponent was college football coach [[Tommy Tuberville]]]]
Jones ran for a full six-year term. He was seen as the most vulnerable senator from either party since Alabama is a deeply Republican state and the circumstances and controversy surrounding his Republican opponent in 2017 were no longer a factor.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Arkin |first1=James |date=February 20, 2019 |title=GOP congressman jumps into critical Alabama Senate race |work=[[Politico]] |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2019/02/20/bradley-byrne-doug-jones-alabama-1193321 |access-date=June 19, 2022}}</ref>

The Democratic Party nominated Jones for the seat unopposed.<ref>{{Cite web |title=United States Senate election in Alabama, 2020 (March 3 Democratic primary) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/United_States_Senate_election_in_Alabama,_2020_(March_3_Democratic_primary) |website=Ballotpedia}}</ref> The two top contenders in the Republican primary were former football coach [[Tommy Tuberville]] and former [[United States Attorney General]] [[Jeff Sessions]], who had held Jones's seat before resigning to become attorney general in 2017. [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. Representative]] [[Bradley Byrne]] was also a contender, sometimes even outpolling the other candidates, but in the first round of the primary, on March 3, Tuberville and Sessions finished second and first. Since neither had a majority of the vote, they advanced to a runoff, which Tuberville won.

Tuberville won the general election 60% to 40%.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mangan |first=Dan |date=November 4, 2020 |title=Tommy Tuberville projected to win Alabama Senate race over incumbent Sen. Doug Jones, a pickup for Republicans |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2020/11/03/alabama-senate-election-results.html |website=CNBC}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=November 3, 2020 |title=Alabama Election Results |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/11/03/us/elections/results-alabama.html |via=NYTimes.com}}</ref> Jones was the only Democratic senator to lose re-election in 2020.<ref>{{Cite news |date=October 28, 2020 |title=Alabama U.S. Senate Primary Election Results |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/03/03/us/elections/results-alabama-senate-primary-election.html |via=NYTimes.com}}</ref>

===Tenure===
Jones was sworn in on January 3, 2018, alongside fellow Democrat [[Tina Smith]] of [[Minnesota]], and his term ran through January 3, 2021, the balance of Sessions's term.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/powerpost/doug-jones-expected-to-take-senate-oath-on-wednesday-shrinking-gop-majority/2018/01/03/b77c1d40-f094-11e7-b3bf-ab90a706e175_story.html |title=Doug Jones is sworn in, shrinking GOP Senate majority |last1=Weigel |first1=David |author-link1=David Weigel |date=2018-01-03 |newspaper=The Washington Post |last2=Sullivan |first2=Sean |access-date=2018-01-03}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/latest-polls-open-alabamas-closely-watched-election-51736753|title=The Latest: Moore not conceding Senate race to Jones|date=December 13, 2017|agency=Associated Press|work=ABC News|access-date=December 13, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171215214905/https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/latest-polls-open-alabamas-closely-watched-election-51736753|archive-date=December 15, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> He was the first Democrat to represent the state in the U.S. Senate in 21 years, and the first elected in 25.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/entry/doug-jones-wins_us_5a3026a9e4b091ca26833755|title=Alabama Elects Doug Jones, The State's First Democratic Senator In 25 Years|date=December 12, 2017|first1=Amanda|last1=Terkel|first2=Andy|last2=Campbell|work=The Huffington Post|access-date=December 13, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.al.com/news/index.ssf/2018/01/doug_jones_swearing-in_ceremon.html |title=Doug Jones swearing-in: Watch live as Senate seats new Alabama member |date=January 3, 2018 |publisher=AL.com |access-date=2018-01-03}}</ref> Jones was one of five Democratic senators who voted for the continuing resolution that failed to pass and consequently led to the [[January 2018 United States federal government shutdown]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/01/19/us/politics/live-senate-vote-government-shutdown.html|title=How Every Senator Voted on the Government Shutdown|last=Lee|first=Jasmine C.|date=2018|work=The New York Times|access-date=2018-01-22}}</ref> According to [[Morning Consult]], which polls approval ratings of senators, {{as of|2019|October|17|lc=y|df=US}}, Jones had a 41% approval rating, with 36% disapproving. This trailed Jones's fellow Alabama senator, Republican Richard Shelby, who had a 45% approval rating, with 30% disapproving.<ref>{{cite web|last=Easley|first=Cameron|url=https://morningconsult.com/senator-rankings/|title=Morning Consult's Senator Approval Rankings|publisher=Morning Consult|date=July 17, 2019|access-date=July 17, 2019}}</ref>

On January 8, 2019, Jones was one of four Democrats to vote to advance a bill imposing sanctions against the Syrian government and furthering U.S. support for Israel and Jordan as Democratic members of the chamber employed tactics to end the [[United States federal government shutdown of 2018–2019]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/424437-democrats-block-foreign-policy-bill-over-shutdown-fight/|title=Democrats block foreign policy bill over shutdown fight|first=Jordain|last=Carney|work=The Hill}}</ref>

In September 2019, after the House launched an [[Impeachment inquiry against Donald Trump|impeachment inquiry]] against President Trump, Jones urged caution on the part of the media and his colleagues because his experience with law had led him to believe that it was "very unlikely there's going to be an absolute smoking gun on either side". He stated his support for "fact-finding" by the House, only after which he would make a decision about Trump's guilt.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2019/09/26/doug-jones-calls-factfinding-probe-allegations-against-trump/3778587002/|title=Doug Jones calls for 'fact-finding' probe of allegations against Trump|date=September 26, 2019|first=Brian|last=Lyman|publisher=Montgomery Advertiser}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Everett |first1=Burgess |title=Trio of Dem senators considering vote to acquit Trump |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2020/01/28/trio-democratic-senators-consider-acquit-trump-108130 |website=Politico |date=January 28, 2020 |access-date=February 6, 2020}}</ref> In February 2020, Jones voted to convict President Donald Trump in his [[First impeachment trial of Donald Trump|impeachment trial]], saying the evidence presented "clearly proves" that Trump used his office to seek to coerce a foreign government to interfere in the election.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2020/02/05/doug-jones-impeachment-vote-110818|title=Red state Democrats stick with party to convict Trump|first1=Marianne|last1=Levine|first2=James|last2=Arkin|website=POLITICO|date=February 5, 2020 |language=en|access-date=2020-02-06}}</ref>

Jones was the only statewide elected Democrat in Alabama and the first Democrat to win statewide office since [[Lucy Baxley]] was elected President of the [[Alabama Public Service Commission]] in 2008. Democrats had not represented Alabama in the U.S. Senate since 1997, when [[Howell Heflin]] left office. Jones was considered a moderate Democrat, who supported [[Reproductive rights|reproductive]] and [[LGBT rights in the United States|LGBT rights]] but demonstrated a willingness to work with Republicans and split with his party on certain issues.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Beaman |first1=Jeremy |title=Sen. Doug Jones has proved himself — so far — to be a moderate Democrat |url=https://yellowhammernews.com/sen-doug-jones-has-proved-himself-so-far-to-be-a-moderate-democrat/ |website=YellowHammer News |date=Apr 13, 2018|access-date=February 6, 2020}}</ref> Jones ran for a full term in [[2020 United States Senate election in Alabama|2020]] and lost to Republican nominee [[Tommy Tuberville]] in a landslide. His margin of defeat was the largest of an incumbent senator since 2010.<ref name="Clare Foran"/>

