==Post-congressional career==
[[File:Doug Jones as Nomination Advisor for Legislative Affairs.jpg|thumb|Jones during his tenure as Nomination Advisor]]
In November 2020, Jones was mentioned as a potential candidate for [[United States Attorney General]] in the [[Cabinet of Joe Biden|Biden administration]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Who Are Contenders for Biden's Cabinet? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/11/11/us/politics/biden-cabinet.html |access-date=November 11, 2020 |work=The New York Times |date=November 11, 2020}}</ref> However, on January 6, 2021, the day after Democrats won the [[2020–21 United States Senate runoff in Georgia|2021 U.S. Senate runoffs in Georgia]], he was informed he would not be given the position. The post was ultimately filled by [[Merrick Garland]].

On January 29, 2021, Jones joined [[CNN]] as a political commentator. He also became a politics fellow at [[Georgetown University]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cbs42.com/alabama-news/doug-jones-named-as-politics-fellow-at-georgetown/|title=Doug Jones named as politics fellow at Georgetown|work=CBS 42 |date=January 20, 2021}}</ref> In May 2021, Jones and his former Senate staff member Cissy Jackson were announced to have joined the Government Relations and Government Enforcement & White Collar division of the D.C.-based law firm [[Arent Fox]], joining the likes of former senator [[Byron Dorgan]] and former representative [[Phil English]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-05-03|title=Former U.S. Sen. Doug Jones joins D.C. law firm|url=https://www.al.com/news/2021/05/former-us-sen-doug-jones-joins-dc-lobbying-firm.html|access-date=2021-12-12|website=al|language=en}}</ref>

On April 20, 2021, the [[Center for American Progress]] announced that Jones would be joining the [[think tank]] as a Distinguished Senior Fellow.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Cusick|first=Julia|date=2021-04-20 |title=RELEASE: CAP Welcomes Senator Doug Jones as a Distinguished Senior Fellow |language=en-US|work=Center for American Progress |url=https://www.americanprogress.org/press/release-cap-welcomes-senator-doug-jones-distinguished-senior-fellow/}}</ref>

In January 2022, Biden named Jones as his "[[Sherpa (political consultant)|sherpa]]" in assisting with the nomination of [[Ketanji Brown Jackson]] to the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] vacancy, filling the vacancy created by the announced retirement of [[Stephen Breyer]].<ref>{{Cite news|last=Rogers|first=Katie|date=2022-02-01|title=White House Chooses Doug Jones to Guide Supreme Court Nominee|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/01/us/politics/doug-jones-supreme-court.html|access-date=2022-02-03|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last1=Levine|first1=Marianne|last2=Everett|first2=Burgess|date=2022-04-05|title=Jackson confirmation battle rejuvenates Doug Jones|language=en-US|work=[[POLITICO]]|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/04/05/doug-jones-ketanji-brown-jackson-scotus-00022891|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Jackson was confirmed by a 53–47 vote on April 7, 2022.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Baker |first=Sam |date=2022-04-07 |title=Ketanji Brown Jackson confirmed as first Black female Supreme Court justice |language=en |url=https://www.axios.com/ketanji-brown-jackson-supreme-court-biden-5aaba226-c0e0-43f6-8952-a803c9c0e29c.html |access-date=2022-04-07}}</ref>

=== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ===
{{Main|2026 Alabama gubernatorial election}}
On November 24, 2025, Jones announced his candidacy for [[governor of Alabama]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Doug Jones launches Alabama governor bid |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5620923-doug-jones-alabama-governor-race-tuberville/ |access-date=November 24, 2025}}</ref> He won the Democratic gubernatorial primary on May 19, 2026, and became the Democratic nominee for governor. He is running against Tommy Tuberville, who he lost his Senate seat to in 2020. This is the first time in U.S. history that a gubernatorial race has been a rematch from a former Senate race.

==Political positions==
The editorial board of ''[[The Birmingham News]]'' has described Jones as a "[[New Democrats (United States)|moderate Democrat]]".<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.al.com/opinion/index.ssf/2017/11/our_view_alabama_voters_must_r.html|title=Our view: Alabama voters must reject Roy Moore; we endorse Doug Jones for U.S. Senate|publisher=AL.com|access-date=November 21, 2017}}</ref> Former [[Alabama Democratic Party]] chair Giles Perkins described Jones as "a moderate, middle-of-the-road guy".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2017/nov/19/doug-jones-roy-moores-opponent-in-alabama-on-verge/|title=Doug Jones, Roy Moore's opponent in Alabama, on verge of history in Senate election|work=The Washington Times|access-date=November 21, 2017}}</ref> Describing his own views, Jones said: "If you look at the positions I've got on health care, if you look at the positions I got on jobs—you should look at the support I have from the business community—I think I'm pretty mainstream."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/alabama-democrat-doug-jones-denies-being-an-ultra-liberal-says-he-opposes-trumps-border-wall/|title=Alabama Democrat Doug Jones denies being an 'ultra-liberal,' says he opposes Trump's border wall|last=Pappas|first=Alex|date=November 18, 2017|website=[[Fox News Channel]]|access-date=November 21, 2017}}</ref> Jones's campaign has emphasized "kitchen-table" issues such as health care and the economy.<ref>{{cite news |first=John |last=Sharp |url=http://www.al.com/news/mobile/index.ssf/2017/10/doug_jones_talks_kitchen_table.html |title=Doug Jones talks 'kitchen table' issues and tax reform at Mobile rally |publisher=AL.com |date=October 18, 2017}}</ref><ref name="LymanTable">{{cite news |first=Brian |last=Lyman |url=http://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/politics/southunionstreet/2017/07/05/alabama-senate-profile-doug-jones-wants-stress-kitchen-table-issues/435601001/ |title=Alabama Senate profile: Doug Jones wants to stress 'kitchen table issues' |work=[[Montgomery Advertiser]] |date=July 7, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/democrats-weigh-best-alabama-senate-candidate-doug-jones/story?id=51166840|title=Democrats weigh how to best help Alabama Senate candidate|author=Parks, Mary Alice|date=November 16, 2017|work=ABC News|access-date=November 20, 2017}}</ref> He has called for bipartisan solutions to those issues<ref name="lyman">{{cite news |last=Lyman |first=Brian |date=November 8, 2017 |title=Doug Jones, Roy Moore talk law enforcement in Montgomery stops |work=Montgomery Advertiser |url=http://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/politics/southunionstreet/2017/11/09/doug-jones-roy-moore-talk-law-enforcement-montgomery-stops/843891001/}}</ref> and pledged to "find common ground" between both major parties.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.economist.com/blogs/democracyinamerica/2017/11/anti-moore|title=Transcript: An interview with Doug Jones|newspaper=The Economist|access-date=November 20, 2017}}</ref> Jones said that people should not "expect [him] to vote solidly for Republicans or Democrats".<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/nationworld/politics/ct-doug-jones-senate-democrats-20171217-story.html|title=Doug Jones says don't expect him to always side with Senate Democrats|last=Yen|first=Hope|work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |date=December 17, 2017|access-date=July 11, 2018}}</ref> During his campaign, he had supporters from both parties, including Republican senator [[Jeff Flake]] of Arizona.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/360190-flake-ill-support-the-democrat-over-moore-in-alabama-senate-race/|title=Flake: I'll support the Democrat over Moore in Alabama Senate race|last=Thomsen|first=Jacqueline|date=November 13, 2017|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|access-date=October 2, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://time.com/5050924/jeff-flake-doug-jones-roy-moore-alabama-senate-race/|title=GOP Senator Jeff Flake Just Wrote a Check to Roy Moore's Democratic Opponent|first=Alana|last=Abramson|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|date=December 5, 2017|access-date=October 2, 2018}}</ref> According to [[FiveThirtyEight]], Jones had voted with President [[Donald Trump]]'s position about 35% of the time as of September 2020.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/congress-trump-score/doug-jones/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180214015019/https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/congress-trump-score/doug-jones/|url-status=dead|archive-date=February 14, 2018|title=Tracking Doug Jones In The Age Of Trump|last=Bycoffe|first=Aaron|date=2017-01-30|publisher=FiveThirtyEight|access-date=2018-03-24}}</ref>

A July 2018 NBC News editorial stated that Jones had voted with Trump more often than all but three of his fellow Democratic senators ([[Joe Manchin]], [[Heidi Heitkamp]] and [[Joe Donnelly]]) while also taking liberal positions more in line with his party, including [[LGBT rights]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/alabama-democrat-doug-jones-walks-tightrope-trump-his-own-party-n887501 |title=Alabama Democrat Doug Jones walks tightrope with Trump and his own party |last1=Edelman |first1=Adam |last2=Shabad |first2=Rebecca |date=July 2, 2018 |website=[[NBC News]] |access-date=December 17, 2018}}</ref>

===Abortion===
Jones is mostly [[Abortion-rights movements|pro-choice]] on [[abortion]] with the exception of late-term abortion stating during a virtual rally "I have never, never supported what is known as a late term abortion." Also in the same virtual rally, he stated "I support the [[Hyde Amendment]] I have said that over and over." In 2018, [[Planned Parenthood]] gave him a 100% rating, while the [[National Right to Life Committee]] gave him a 0% rating. Jones voted against the [[Pain-Capable Unborn Child Protection Act]], which prohibits abortion after 20 weeks except in cases of [[rape]], [[incest]], or danger to the pregnant woman's health.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.al.com/news/index.ssf/2018/01/senate_rejects_20-week_abortio.html|title=Senate rejects 20-week abortion ban; GOP criticizes Jones vote|first=Leada|last=Gore|website=AL.com|date=January 31, 2018|access-date=June 22, 2018}}</ref> He also pledged to support Planned Parenthood as a senator.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bustle.com/p/where-does-doug-jones-stand-on-abortion-his-views-set-him-far-apart-from-his-opponent-7580669|title=Doug Jones Has Made His Stance On Abortion Crystal Clear|last=Holter|first=Lauren|website=[[Bustle (website)|Bustle]]|date=December 13, 2017|access-date=June 22, 2018}}</ref> In May 2019, he criticized the passage of an abortion ban in Alabama, calling it "shameful".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2019/05/16/alabama-abortion-law-democratic-sen-doug-jones-calls-shameful/3695724002/|title=Alabama Democratic Sen. Doug Jones calls state's new restrictive abortion law 'shameful'|first=Deborah Barfield|last=Berry|newspaper=[[USA Today]]|language=en|date=May 16, 2019|access-date=May 16, 2019}}</ref>

In February 2019, Jones was one of three Senate Democrats to vote for the Born-Alive Abortion Survivors Protection Act, a bill requiring health care practitioners present at the time of a birth "exercise the same degree of professional skill, care, and diligence to preserve the life and health of the child as a reasonably diligent and conscientious health care practitioner would render to any other child born alive at the same gestational age."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/senate-to-vote-on-born-alive-bill-to-protect-infants-who-survive-a-failed-abortion|title=Dems block 'born alive' bill to provide medical care to infants who survive failed abortions|first1=Caleb|last1=Parke|first2=Gregg|last2=Re|website=[[Fox News]]|date=February 25, 2019}}</ref>

=== Agriculture ===
On December 11, 2018, Jones voted for the conference farm bill, which included his provisions for farmers, rural health, wastewater infrastructure, and high-speed internet.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.jones.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/senator-doug-jones-champions-rural-alabama-priorities-in-final-farm-bill-legislation|title=Senator Doug Jones Champions Rural Alabama Priorities in Final Farm Bill Legislation|date=December 11, 2018|publisher=Jones.Senate.Gov|access-date=September 1, 2019|archive-date=September 1, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190901011748/https://www.jones.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/senator-doug-jones-champions-rural-alabama-priorities-in-final-farm-bill-legislation|url-status=dead}}</ref> In May 2019, he co-sponsored the Transporting Livestock Across America Safely Act, a bipartisan bill introduced by [[Ben Sasse]] (R-[[Nebraska]]) and [[Jon Tester]] (D-[[Montana]]) intended to reform hours of service for livestock haulers by authorizing drivers to have the flexibility to rest at any point during their trip without it being counted against their hours of service and exempting loading and unloading times from the calculation of driving time.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.dairyherd.com/article/senators-reintroduce-transporting-livestock-across-america-safely-act|title=Senators Reintroduce Transporting Livestock Across America Safely Act|first=Wyatt|last=Bechtel|date=May 1, 2019|website=dailyherd.com}}</ref>

=== Broadband ===
In June 2019, Jones and Senator [[Susan Collins]] (R-[[Maine]]) cosponsored the American Broadband Buildout Act of 2019, a bill that requested $5 billion for a matching funds program that the [[Federal Communications Commission]] (FCC) would administer to "give priority to qualifying projects" and mandated that at least 15% of funding go to high-cost and geographically challenged areas. The legislation also authorized recipients of the funding to form "public awareness" and "digital literacy" campaigns to further awareness of the "value and benefits of broadband internet access service" and served as a companion to the Broadband Data Improvement Act.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.multichannel.com/news/senators-collins-jones-propose-5-billion-plan-for-rural-broadband-buildout|title=Senators Propose $5 Billion Plan for Rural Broadband Buildout|first=Gary|last=Arlen|website=multichannel.com|date=June 28, 2019}}</ref>

=== Criminal justice reform ===
In December 2018, Jones voted for the [[First Step Act]], legislation aimed at reducing recidivism rates among federal prisoners by expanding job training and other programs in addition to expanding early-release programs and modifying sentencing laws such as mandatory minimum sentences for nonviolent drug offenders, "to more equitably punish drug offenders."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/18/us/politics/senate-criminal-justice-bill.html|title=Senate Passes Bipartisan Criminal Justice Bill|first=Nicholas|last=Fandos|date=December 18, 2018|work=[[The New York Times]]|quote=In one of this Congress's final acts, every Democrat and all but 12 Republicans voted in favor of the legislation — an outcome that looked highly unlikely this month amid skepticism from Republican leaders.}}</ref>

Jones supports the reversal of [[Mandatory sentencing|mandatory]] [[three-strikes law]]s for nonviolent offenses to give judges flexibility in giving sentences.<ref name="LymanTable"/>

=== Corporate disclosure ===
In June 2019, along with [[Mark Warner]] (D-[[Virginia]]), [[Tom Cotton]] (R-[[Arkansas]]), and [[Mike Rounds]] (R-[[South Dakota]]), Jones introduced the Improving Laundering Laws and Increasing Comprehensive Information Tracking of Criminal Activity in Shell Holdings (ILLICIT CASH) Act, a bill mandating that [[shell company|shell companies]] disclose their real owners to the [[United States Department of the Treasury|Treasury Department]] and updating outdated federal anti-[[money laundering]] laws by bettering communications among law enforcement, regulatory agencies, the financial industry, and the industry and regulators of advanced technology. Jones said he was "all too familiar with criminals hiding behind shell corporations to enable their illegal behavior" from being an attorney.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://kfgo.com/news/articles/2019/jun/10/us-senators-launch-bill-to-broaden-shell-companies-disclosures/|title=U.S. senators launch bill to broaden shell companies' disclosures|first=Mark|last=Hosenball|date=June 10, 2019|website=kfgo.com|access-date=July 16, 2019|archive-date=July 16, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190716184739/https://kfgo.com/news/articles/2019/jun/10/us-senators-launch-bill-to-broaden-shell-companies-disclosures/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== Defense ===
In March 2018, Jones voted against [[Bernie Sanders]]'s and [[Chris Murphy]]'s resolution to end U.S. support for the [[Saudi Arabian-led intervention in Yemen]].<ref>{{cite news |author=Iannelli, Jerry |date=March 21, 2018 |title=Sen. Bill Nelson Votes to Continue Helping Saudi Arabia Kill Yemeni Citizens |work=Miami News Times |url=https://www.miaminewtimes.com/news/florida-senator-bill-nelson-votes-for-yemen-war-10192904}}</ref>

In an interview with ''[[The Birmingham News]]'', Jones said he favored increasing defense spending, saying it would boost Alabama's local economy, particularly in the areas around [[NASA]]'s [[Marshall Space Flight Center]] and the U.S. Army's [[Redstone Arsenal]], and protect the United States from foreign threats.<ref>{{Cite news |author=Gattis, Paul |date=November 8, 2017 |title=Doug Jones: Strong national defense 'incredibly important' |publisher=AL.com |url=http://www.al.com/news/huntsville/index.ssf/2017/11/doug_jones_strong_national_def.html}}</ref>

Jones voted to confirm [[Mike Pompeo]] as [[United States Secretary of State|Secretary of State]], joining with Republicans and five other Democratic senators. He opposed [[Gina Haspel]]'s nomination as [[Director of the Central Intelligence Agency|CIA director]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2018-05-16 |title=Doug Jones opposes Trump's CIA nominee |work=Alabama Daily News |url=http://www.aldailynews.com/doug-jones-opposes-trumps-cia-nominee/ |access-date=2018-07-11}}</ref>

In May 2019, Jones co-sponsored the South China Sea and East China Sea Sanctions Act, a bipartisan bill reintroduced by [[Marco Rubio]] (R-[[Florida]]) and [[Ben Cardin]] (D-[[Maryland]]) intended to disrupt China's consolidation or expansion of its claims of jurisdiction over the sea and air space in disputed zones in the South China Sea.<ref>{{cite news |last=Ghosh |first=Nirmal |date=May 24, 2019 |title=US Bill reintroduced to deter China in South China, East China seas |publisher=The Straits Times |url=https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/bill-introduced-to-deter-china-in-south-china-east-china-seas}}</ref>

In August 2019, after Representatives [[Rashida Tlaib]] (D-[[Michigan]]) and [[Ilhan Omar]] (D-[[Minnesota]]) were denied entry into [[Israel]] due to their support for [[Boycott, Divestment and Sanctions]] (BDS), Jones said he was "concerned the relationship with Israel is beginning to see some cracks for political reasons" and that the US-Israel relationship was being "used as a political weapon to try to divide people for political gain" in both countries. He added that while he did not agree "with a lot of their views on Israel", Tlaib and Omar were entitled to them, and cited the necessity of having to defend other members of Congress when they are barred from "the right to go and visit with other members".<ref>{{cite news |author=Thornton, William |date=August 20, 2019 |title=Doug Jones decries using U.S.-Israel relations 'as political weapon' |publisher=AL.com |url=https://www.al.com/news/birmingham/2019/08/doug-jones-decries-using-us-israel-relations-as-political-weapon.html}}</ref>

In October 2019, Jones was one of six senators to sign a bipartisan letter to President Trump calling on him to "urge Turkey to end their offensive and find a way to a peaceful resolution while supporting our Kurdish partners to ensure regional stability" and arguing that to leave Syria without installing protections for American allies would endanger both them and the US.<ref>{{cite news |author=Koplowitz, Howard |date=October 17, 2019 |title=Doug Jones joins bipartisan group of senators in urging Trump to rethink Syria policy |publisher=al.com |url=https://www.al.com/news/2019/10/doug-jones-joins-bipartisan-group-of-senators-in-urging-trump-to-rethink-syria-policy.html}}</ref>

===Economy===
''[[Newsweek]]'' has described Jones as an [[Populism|economic populist]].<ref>{{Cite news |author=Porter, Tom |date=September 27, 2017 |title=Who is Doug Jones, the KKK-fighting Democrat taking on far-right Roy Moore in the Alabama Senate race? |work=Newsweek |url=http://www.newsweek.com/who-doug-jones-kkk-fighting-democrat-attorney-roy-moore-alabama-senate-672255 |access-date=November 11, 2017}}</ref> He was one of five Democrats to vote for the Republican budget deal in January 2018<ref>{{Cite news |author=Gore, Leada |date=January 20, 2018 |title=Sen. Doug Jones votes for Republican-backed budget deal |publisher=AL.com |url=http://www.al.com/news/index.ssf/2018/01/sen_doug_jones_will_vote_for_r.html |access-date=2018-01-26}}</ref> and one of 17 Democrats to vote with Republicans in favor of a bill to ease banking regulations.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Schoen |first1=Jacob |last2=Pramuk |first2=John W. |date=2018-03-15 |title=Why 17 Democrats voted with Republicans to ease bank rules |publisher=CNBC |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/03/15/why-senate-democrats-voted-for-bank-bill-to-ease-dodd-frank-rules.html |access-date=2018-06-22}}</ref> Jones opposes the tariffs imposed by the [[First presidency of Donald Trump|first Trump administration]].<ref>{{Cite news |author=Hrynkiw, Ivana |date=July 24, 2018 |title=Sen. Doug Jones tweets back at Trump: Tariffs are the 'worst' |publisher=AL.com |url=https://www.al.com/news/birmingham/index.ssf/2018/07/sen_doug_jones_tweets_back_at.html |access-date=2018-08-02}}</ref>

=== Education ===
In February 2019, Jones was one of 20 senators to sponsor the Employer Participation in Repayment Act, enabling employers to contribute up to $5,250 to the student loans of their employees.<ref>{{cite news |last=Varnier |first=Julia |date=February 13, 2019 |title=Warner, Thune introduce legislation to address student debt crisis |publisher=wtkr.com |url=https://wtkr.com/2019/02/13/warner-thune-introduce-legislation-to-address-student-debt-crisis/}}</ref>

In July 2019, Jones and [[Tina Smith]] (D-[[Minnesota]]) introduced the Addressing Teacher Shortages Act, a bill to allow [[school districts]] across the United States to apply for grants to aid the schools in attracting and retaining quality teachers. The bill also funded the Education Department's efforts to help smaller and under-resourced districts apply for grants.<ref>{{cite news |date=July 31, 2019 |title=Smith co-introduces bill to boost sagging teacher numbers across country |publisher=Brainerd Dispatch |url=https://www.brainerddispatch.com/news/government-and-politics/4048107-Smith-co-introduces-bill-to-boost-sagging-teacher-numbers-across-country}}</ref>

On September 19, 2019, Jones took to the Senate floor to request unanimous consent to pass legislation that would further the $255 million in federal funding for minority-serving colleges and universities ahead of its expiration date in weeks. The vote was shut down by Senate Education Committee Chairman [[Lamar Alexander]] (R-[[Tennessee]]), who instead called for support for the passage of "a long-term solution that will provide certainty to college presidents and their students" and "a few additional bipartisan higher education proposals."<ref>{{cite news |last=Douglas-Gabriel |first=Danielle |date=September 19, 2019 |title=Lamar Alexander blocks vote on funding for minority-serving colleges |publisher=SFGate |url=https://www.sfgate.com/news/article/Lamar-Alexander-blocks-vote-on-funding-for-14453619.php}}</ref>

=== Environment ===
In March 2019, Jones was one of three Democrats to vote with all Senate Republicans against the [[Green New Deal]] when it came up for a procedural vote. All other Senate Democrats voted "present" on the legislation, a move anticipated as allowing them to avoid having a formal position.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Carney |first1=Jordain |last2=Green |first2=Miranda |date=March 26, 2019 |title=Senate blocks Green New Deal |work=The Hill |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/435899-senate-blocks-green-new-deal/}}</ref>

In June 2019, Jones was one of 44 senators to introduce the International Climate Accountability Act, legislation that would prevent Trump from using funds in an attempt to withdraw from the [[Paris Agreement]] and directing the Trump administration to instead develop a strategic plan for the United States that would allow it to meet its commitment under the Paris Agreement.<ref>{{cite news |date=June 10, 2019 |title=Oregon senators call on Trump to honor climate agreement |publisher=ktvz.com |url=https://www.ktvz.com/news/oregon-senators-call-on-trump-to-honor-climate-agreement/1085035967 |access-date=June 16, 2019 |archive-date=June 11, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190611130357/https://www.ktvz.com/news/oregon-senators-call-on-trump-to-honor-climate-agreement/1085035967 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Gun policy===
Jones supports some [[gun control]] measures, including "tighter background checks for gun sales and to raise the age requirement to purchase a gun from 18 to 21",<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.rollcall.com/news/politics/alabama-ready-gun-control-sen-doug-jones-says|title=Alabama Ready for More Gun Control, Sen. Doug Jones Says|last1=Connolly|first1=Griffin|date=March 5, 2018|newspaper=[[Roll Call]]|access-date=June 22, 2018|archive-date=April 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412225253/http://www.rollcall.com/news/politics/alabama-ready-gun-control-sen-doug-jones-says|url-status=dead}}</ref>  but has said that he does not support an assault weapons ban and that such a ban could not pass Congress.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/sunday-talk-shows/381168-democrat-a-gun-ban-is-not-feasible-right-now/|title=Democrat: A gun ban is not 'feasible right now'|last=Sanchez|first=Luis|date=April 1, 2018|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|access-date=June 22, 2018}}</ref> Jones himself is a gun owner.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.al.com/news/index.ssf/2018/03/it_is_time_doug_jones_voices_s.html?__vfz=rtw_top_pages=3202600012274|title='It is time': Doug Jones calls on Senate to unite on stemming gun violence in floor speech|first=Howard|last=Koplowitz|website=AL.com|date=March 21, 2018|access-date=June 22, 2018}}</ref>

In March 2018, Jones was one of 10 senators to sign a letter to Chairman of the [[United States Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor and Pensions]] [[Lamar Alexander]] and ranking Democrat [[Patty Murray]] requesting they schedule a hearing on the causes and remedies of mass shootings in the wake of the [[Stoneman Douglas High School shooting]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/blogs/floor-action/senate/380313-senate-democrats-request-hearing-on-school-shootings/|title=Senate Dems request health panel hearing on school shootings|first=Jordain|last=Carney|date=March 26, 2018|newspaper=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref>

In 2018, Jones co-sponsored the NICS Denial Notification Act,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wfmynews2.com/article/news/nation-now/school-safety-bill-introduced-by-bipartisan-senators-in-response-to-florida-shooting/465-12ad93e2-e807-4f78-aafe-8df29fcb6ab0|title=School safety bill introduced by bipartisan senators in response to Florida shooting|date=March 5, 2018|first=Nicole|last=Gaudiano|publisher=wfmynews2.com|access-date=June 16, 2019|archive-date=February 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210204162507/https://www.wfmynews2.com/article/news/nation-now/school-safety-bill-introduced-by-bipartisan-senators-in-response-to-florida-shooting/465-12ad93e2-e807-4f78-aafe-8df29fcb6ab0|url-status=dead}}</ref> legislation developed in the aftermath of the [[Stoneman Douglas High School shooting]] that would require federal authorities to inform states within a day after a person failing the National Instant Criminal Background Check System attempts to buy a firearm.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.seacoastonline.com/news/20180310/collins-backed-push-to-keep-criminals-from-guns-progresses|title=Collins-backed push to keep criminals from guns progresses|date=March 10, 2018|website=seacoastonline.com}}</ref>

===Healthcare===
Jones opposes the repeal of the [[Affordable Care Act]], but he has called for changes to the U.S. health-care system, which he calls broken.<ref name="Gattis">{{cite news |last=Gattis |first=Paul |date=November 6, 2017 |title=Doug Jones pledges to 'fix broken health care' in new Senate campaign ad |publisher=AL.com |url=http://www.al.com/news/huntsville/index.ssf/2017/11/doug_jones_pledges_to_fix_brok.html}}</ref> He supports the reauthorization of the [[Children's Health Insurance Program]]<ref name="Gattis" /> and during his senatorial campaign repeatedly criticized [[Roy Moore|his opponent]] for lacking a clear stance on the program.<ref name="Gattis" /><ref name="lyman" /> Jones says he is open to the idea of a [[Public health insurance option|public option]], but that he is "not there yet" on [[single-payer healthcare]].<ref name="LymanTable" /> In January 2018, Jones was one of six Democrats to join most Republicans in voting to confirm [[Alex Azar]], Trump's nominee for [[United States Secretary of Health and Human Services|Secretary of Health and Human Services]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Senate confirms Alex Azar as Trump's new health secretary |work=ABC News |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Health/wireStory/senate-confirms-alex-azar-trumps-2nd-health-secretary-52585225 |access-date=2018-01-26}}</ref>

In December 2018, Jones was one of 42 senators to sign a letter to Trump administration officials [[Alex Azar]], [[Seema Verma]], and [[Steve Mnuchin]] arguing that the administration was improperly using Section 1332 of the Affordable Care Act to authorize states to "increase health care costs for millions of consumers while weakening protections for individuals with preexisting conditions". The senators requested the administration withdraw the policy and "re-engage with stakeholders, states, and Congress".<ref>{{cite news |date=December 20, 2018 |title=U.S. Senator Tammy Baldwin Calls on Trump Administration to Stop Pushing Health Insurance Plans that Weaken Pre-Existing Condition Protections |publisher=urbanmilwaukee.com |url=https://urbanmilwaukee.com/pressrelease/u-s-senator-tammy-baldwin-calls-on-trump-administration-to-stop-pushing-health-insurance-plans-that-weaken-pre-existing-condition-protections/}}</ref>

In January 2019, Jones was one of six senators to cosponsor the Health Insurance Tax Relief Act, delaying the Health Insurance Tax for two years.<ref>{{cite news |date=January 21, 2019 |title=Shaheen introduces bill that would delay health insurance tax |publisher=mychamplainvalley.com |url=https://www.mychamplainvalley.com/news/local-news/shaheen-introduces-bill-that-would-delay-health-insurance-tax/1713236203}}</ref>

In January 2019, Jones was one of six Democratic senators to introduce the American Miners Act of 2019, a bill that would amend the [[Surface Mining Control and Reclamation Act of 1977]] to swap funds in excess of the amounts needed to meet existing obligations under the Abandoned Mine Land fund to the 1974 Pension Plan as part of an effort to prevent its insolvency as a result of coal company bankruptcies and the 2008 financial crisis. It also increased the [[Black Lung Benefits Act of 1972#Black Lung Disability Trust Fund|Black Lung Disability Trust Fund]] tax and ensured that miners affected by the 2018 coal company bankruptcies would not lose their health care.<ref>{{cite news |last=Holdren |first=Wendy |date=January 4, 2019 |title=Legislation introduced to secure miners pensions and health care |publisher=The Register-Herald |url=https://www.register-herald.com/health/legislation-introduced-to-secure-miners-pensions-and-health-care/article_3dea66bd-f74d-520b-abba-4bb600537361.html}}</ref>

In January 2019, during the [[2018–19 United States federal government shutdown]], Jones was one of 34 senators to sign a letter to [[Commissioner of Food and Drugs]] [[Scott Gottlieb]] recognizing the efforts of the FDA to address the effect of the government shutdown on public health and employees while remaining alarmed "that the continued shutdown will result in increasingly harmful effects on the agency's employees and the safety and security of the nation's food and medical products".<ref>{{cite news |date=January 15, 2019 |title=Democratic Senators "Alarmed" by Shutdown's Potential Impact on Food Safety |publisher=foodsafetymagazine.com |url=https://www.foodsafetymagazine.com/news/democratic-senators-e2809calarmede2809d-by-shutdowne28099s-potential-impact-on-food-safety/}}</ref>

In February 2019, Jones was one of 11 senators to sign a letter to insulin manufactures [[Eli Lilly and Company]], [[Novo Nordisk]], and [[Sanofi]] over increased insulin prices and charging that the price increases caused patients to lack "access to the life-saving medications they need".<ref>{{cite news |date=February 5, 2019 |title=Sen. Kaine calls on pharmaceutical companies to explain skyrocketing insulin prices |publisher=WVEC |url=https://www.13newsnow.com/article/news/politics/sen-kaine-calls-on-pharmaceutical-companies-to-explain-skyrocketing-insulin-prices/291-e403ed97-1b9a-4092-87ff-4126fa25036d}}</ref>

In September 2019, amid discussions to prevent a government shutdown, Jones was one of six Democratic senators to sign a letter to congressional leadership advocating the passage of legislation to permanently fund health care and pension benefits for retired coal miners as "families in Virginia, West Virginia, Wyoming, Alabama, Colorado, North Dakota and New Mexico" would start to receive notifications of health care termination by the end of the following month.<ref>{{cite news |date=September 16, 2019 |title=Manchin, colleagues send letter urging permanent funding for miners health care, pensions |publisher=wvmetronews.com |url=http://wvmetronews.com/2019/09/16/manchin-colleagues-send-letter-urging-permanent-funding-for-miners-health-care-pensions/}}</ref>

In October 2019, Jones was one of 27 senators to sign a letter to Senate Majority Leader [[Mitch McConnell]] (R-[[Kentucky]]) and Senate Minority Leader [[Chuck Schumer]] (D-[[New York (state)|New York]]) advocating the passage of the Community Health Investment, Modernization, and Excellence (CHIME) Act, which was set to expire the following month. The senators warned that if the funding for the Community Health Center Fund (CHCF) was allowed to expire, it "would cause an estimated 2,400 site closures, 47,000 lost jobs, and threaten the health care of approximately 9 million Americans."<ref>{{cite news |date=October 23, 2019 |title=U.S. Senator Tammy Baldwin Working to Extend Long Term Funding for Community Health Centers |publisher=Urban Milwaukee |url=https://urbanmilwaukee.com/pressrelease/u-s-senator-tammy-baldwin-working-to-extend-long-term-funding-for-community-health-centers/}}</ref>

=== Immigration ===
In 2018, Jones participated in votes concerning immigration and [[Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals]] (DACA). He voted in favor of the [[John McCain|McCain]]–[[Chris Coons|Coons]] proposal to offer a pathway to citizenship to undocumented immigrants brought to the United States as children, known as [[DREAM Act|Dreamers]], which did not include funding for a border wall; voted against withholding federal funding from sanctuary cities; voted for [[Susan Collins]]'s bipartisan bill to offer a pathway to citizenship and federal funding for border security; and voted against Trump's proposal to offer a pathway to citizenship while reducing overall legal immigration numbers and using federal funds for a border wall.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/02/16/heres-how-your-senators-voted-on-failed-immigration-proposals.html|title=How your senators voted on failed immigration proposals|last=Schoen|first=John W.|date=2018-02-16|publisher=CNBC |access-date=2019-01-02}}</ref> He has also proposed reassessing the current quota system.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://immigrationimpact.com/2017/12/13/doug-jones-immigration-policy/|title=Where Doug Jones Stands on Immigration Policy|date=2017-12-13|publisher=Immigration Impact|access-date=2019-01-30}}</ref> He has agreed that improvements in border security are needed but does not believe it is a national emergency.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.al.com/news/2019/01/sen-doug-jones-calls-trump-proposal-hopeful-sign.html|title=Sen. Doug Jones calls Trump proposal 'hopeful sign'|last=Gattis|first=Paul|date=2019-01-19|publisher=Al.com|language=en-US|access-date=2019-03-20}}</ref>

===LGBT rights===
Jones supports [[same-sex marriage]] and said that his son Carson, who is gay, helped change his views.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.advocate.com/politics/2018/4/12/alabama-sen-doug-jones-says-gay-son-led-him-more-pro-lgbt|title=Alabama Sen. Doug Jones Says Gay Son Led Him to Be More Pro-LGBT|author=Sobel, Ariel|date=April 12, 2018|publisher=Advocate|access-date=July 11, 2018}}</ref> In 2017, he was endorsed by the [[Human Rights Campaign]], which supports [[LGBT]] rights.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://votesmart.org/candidate/evaluations/176464/doug-jones|title=Doug Jones' Ratings and Endorsements|publisher=Vote Smart}}</ref> Jones supports protections for transgender students and transgender troops.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2017/12/13/politics/doug-jones/index.html|title=Who is Doug Jones, who just won in Alabama?|first1=Aileen|last1= Graef|first2= Caroline |last2=Kenny|publisher=CNN|access-date=2018-07-11}}</ref>

=== United States Postal Service ===
In March 2019, Jones co-sponsored a bipartisan resolution led by [[Gary Peters]] (D-[[Michigan]]) and [[Jerry Moran]] (R-[[Kansas]]) that opposed privatization of the [[United States Postal Service]] (USPS), citing the USPS as a self-sustaining establishment and noting concerns that privatization could cause higher prices and reduced services for USPS customers, especially in rural communities.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.uppermichiganssource.com/content/news/Peters-Moran-opposing-privatization-of-USPS-506834151.html|title=Peters, Moran reintroduce bipartisan resolution opposing privatization of USPS|date=March 7, 2019|publisher=uppermichiganssource.com}}</ref>
[[File:Widows Tax press conference.jpg|thumb|Jones speaking in support of eliminating the Widows Tax in 2019.]]

===Taxes===
Jones has not called for tax increases and has instead called for reductions in corporate taxes "to try to get reinvestment back into this country".<ref name="PolitiFactTrumpLie">{{Cite news|url=http://www.politifact.com/truth-o-meter/statements/2017/nov/28/donald-trump/donald-trump-falsely-claims-doug-jones-wants-raise/|title=Donald Trump wrongly claims Doug Jones wants to raise taxes|author=Kruzel, John|publisher=PolitiFact|date=November 28, 2017|access-date=December 2, 2017}}</ref> He opposed the [[Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017]], calling it fiscally irresponsible and skewed to benefit the wealthy while ignoring or hurting the middle class.<ref name="PolitiFactTrumpLie" />

In 2019, along with fellow Democrat [[Amy Klobuchar]] and Republicans [[Pat Toomey]] and [[Bill Cassidy]], Jones was a lead sponsor of the Gold Star Family Tax Relief Act, a bill to undo a provision in the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act that raised the tax on the benefit children receive from a parent's Department of Defense survivor benefits plan to 37% from an average of 12% to 15%. The bill passed in the Senate in May 2019.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.brainerddispatch.com/news/government-and-politics/4616791-klobuchar-bill-protecting-gold-star-families-trump-tax-hike|title=Klobuchar bill protecting Gold Star families from Trump tax hike passes Senate|date=May 23, 2019|publisher=Brainerd Dispatch}}</ref>

=== Trade ===
In 2018, along with Republicans [[Joni Ernst]] and [[Rob Portman]], Jones introduced the Trade Security Act, a bill that would modify Section 232 of the Trade Expansion Act of 1962 to require that the Defense Department justify the national-security basis for new tariffs under Section 232 and implement an increase of congressional oversight of the process. Jones said the process currently led by the Commerce Department to investigate whether a trading partner is undermining U.S. national security had "been misused to target important job-creating industries in Alabama like auto manufacturing" and that the bill would refocus "efforts on punishing bad actors, rather than hurting American manufacturers, workers, and consumers."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://altoday.com/archives/25515-doug-jones-introduces-bipartisan-bill-to-reform-process-for-national-security-tariffs-increase-oversight|title=Doug Jones introduces bipartisan bill to reform process for national security tariffs, increase oversight|first=Elizabeth|last=Patton|date=August 1, 2018|publisher=Alabama Today}}</ref>

In December 2018, Jones stated that automakers and soybean farmers were fearful of the Trump administration's trade policy and added that his constituents in Alabama were questioning Trump's success.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/421228-doug-jones-car-makers-scared-to-death-over-trump-tariffs/|title=Doug Jones: Carmakers 'scared to death' over Trump tariffs|author=Bowden, John|date=December 13, 2018|work=The Hill}}</ref>

In February 2019, amid a report by the Commerce Department that ZTE had been caught illegally shipping goods of American origin to Iran and North Korea, Jones was one of seven senators to sponsor a bill reimposing sanctions on ZTE in the event that ZTE did not honor both American laws and its agreement with the Trump administration.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-trade-china-zte/u-s-lawmakers-target-chinas-zte-with-sanctions-bill-idUSKCN1PU2MU|title=U.S. lawmakers target China's ZTE with sanctions bill|date=February 5, 2019|publisher=Reuters}}</ref>

In a July 2019 committee hearing, Jones predicted that tariffs would eventually directly hit the consumer and they would witness "tariffs that are going to cause a depletion in supply of things like Bibles and artificial fishing lures, which are fairly standard staples in Alabama."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://fox17.com/news/nation-world/top-trump-advisor-china-is-on-the-wrong-side-of-history-if-they-reject-trade-deal|title=Top Trump advisor: China is on the 'wrong side of history' if they reject trade deal|date=July 17, 2019|first=James|last=Rosen|publisher=fox17.com}}</ref>

Addressing the North Alabama International Trade Association in September 2019, Jones said Alabama had a fairly robust economy that was also "pretty fragile and it could go completely bust if we don't get this trade war with China and other trade issues resolved and resolved soon", and that uncertainty about tariffs was affecting business confidence.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://whnt.com/2019/09/05/sen-doug-jones-talks-about-china-trade-war-with-north-alabama-business-owners/|title=Sen. Doug Jones talks about China trade war with North Alabama business owners|first=Jordan|last=Dafnis|date=September 5, 2019|publisher=whnt.com}}</ref>

=== Veterans ===
In December 2018, Jones was one of 21 senators to sign a letter to [[United States Secretary of Veterans Affairs|Veterans Affairs Secretary]] [[Robert Wilkie]] calling it "appalling that the VA is not conducting oversight of its own outreach efforts" in spite of suicide prevention being the VA's highest clinical priority and requesting that Wilkie "consult with experts with proven track records of successful public and mental health outreach campaigns with a particular emphasis on how those individuals measure success".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://urbanmilwaukee.com/pressrelease/u-s-senator-tammy-baldwin-presses-va-for-answers-on-misuse-of-suicide-prevention-funds/|title=U.S. Senator Tammy Baldwin Presses VA for Answers on Misuse Of Suicide Prevention Funds|date=January 4, 2019|publisher=urbanmilwaukee.com}}</ref>

==Personal life==
[[File:Terri Sewell with Doug and Louise Jones.jpg|thumb|180px|Doug and Louise Jones with [[Terri Sewell]] in January 2018]]
Jones married Louise New on December 12, 1992.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://dougjonesforsenate.com//|title=Meet Doug Jones": "Doug is married to the former Louise New from Cullman, Alabama. They will celebrate their 25th anniversary the night of the Special Election in December. |publisher=Doug Jones for Senate |date=August 16, 2017|access-date=August 16, 2017}}</ref> They have three children.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://dougjonesforsenate.com/|title=Meet Doug Jones|last=Jones|first=Doug|date=August 16, 2017|publisher=Doug Jones for Senate|url-status=dead|access-date=August 16, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171212011108/https://dougjonesforsenate.com/|archive-date=December 12, 2017}}</ref> Jones' father died of [[dementia]] on December 28, 2019.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://abc3340.com/news/local/father-of-alabama-sen-doug-jones-dies-after-dementia-fight|title=Father of Alabama Sen. Doug Jones dies after dementia fight|website=abc3340.com|publisher=The Associated Press|date=2020-01-03|access-date=2020-01-03}}</ref>

Jones has been a member of the [[United Methodist Church|Canterbury United Methodist Church]] in [[Mountain Brook, Alabama|Mountain Brook]] for more than 33 years.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.al.com/news/index.ssf/2017/09/son_of_a_steelworker_doug_jone.html|title=Son of a steelworker, Doug Jones works to connect with Alabama voters|last=Garrison|first=Greg|date=September 28, 2017 |publisher=AL.com|access-date=November 12, 2017}}</ref> He also serves on the advisory board of the Blackburn Institute, a leadership development and civic engagement program at the University of Alabama.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blackburninstitute.sa.ua.edu/people/advisory-board/ |title=Advisory Board |publisher=Blackburn Institute |access-date=September 11, 2018}}</ref>

